From 23cd22828e2a7917f689d0b52b5b091314773058 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 15:35:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] A paused turn the startup sweep will not take is
 owed to no successor; automation replays recheck past the yield window

Follow-up to the review of #1435 (t_01a1cfcca83f9266), two non-blocking findings:

- update_pause.successor_owes_continuation reported a dead writer's pause as owed for the first
  SUCCESSOR_YIELD_S of every newly started process, whatever its age: a 2 h old pause (past the
  sweep's freshness window) or one out of attempts delayed the chat's headless deliveries by up to
  300 s after each restart, though no successor would ever continue it. It now returns False when
  the startup sweep would skip the marker (startup_resume._skip_reason with the sweep's own
  _settings) or the sweep is off, so the two cannot drift. No new age constant.
- automation_replay rechecked a "busy" wake 10 x 60 s = 600 s, less than SUCCESSOR_YIELD_UNKNOWN_S
  (660 s): a replay first fired just after a dead writer's pause stamp gave up before the window
  ended. BUSY_RECHECKS_MAX is now derived from the window plus one recheck interval (12).

Tests (test_cold_resume_yields_paused_turn.py, real session.resume path): a stale or
out-of-attempts pause with a reader started 10 s ago is not owed and the wake resumes the chat; a
pause the sweep takes is owed; a busy replay is still pending when the window ends and delivers on
its next fire. All three fail on the parent. test_wake_yields_paused_turn.py patches the writer
probe where the sweep's check reads it.
